          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            In the theory of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/programming+languages"} programming languages
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functions"} functions
            is useful for compositional reasoning, but at first seems to rule out programming with more sophisticated
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monad+in+computer+science"} control structures
            which can actually be very useful in practice. For example, we might want to write an integer division routine which raises an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exception"} exception
            on division-by-zero, or a procedure that lazily computes the first satisfying assignment to a boolean formula while giving the user the ability to query to obtain additional ones.
          %p Now, an “impure” functional language such as OCaml lets us do some of this directly. For example, we can write the following code for integer division:
          %pre
            %code
              :preserve
                let divide : int -> int -> int =
                  fun x y -> if y == 0 then raise Division_by_zero else x / y          %p
            However, it should be said that the type ascription “
            %code> int -&gt; int -&gt; int
            ” is a bit misleading here (or at least open to misinterpretation), since
            %code divide
            cannot actually be modelled as a set-theoretic function of type
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mo →
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mo →
                    %mi ℤ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z} \to \mathbb{Z} \to \mathbb{Z}
          %p
            %em Continuation-passing style
            (or CPS) is a very general technique that allows one to express many different seemingly “non-functional” patterns of control flow within the confines of pure functional programming. The basic idea is that a procedure in CPS takes an additional argument (often named “
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            ”) representing the
            = succeed "," do
              %em continuation
            = succeed ")," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi v
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi v
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k(v)
          %p
            To transform the example above into continuation-passing style, first we must fix an “answer type”, that is, the return type of the continuation. In this case, to allow for the possibility of raising a division-by-zero error, the answer type should be a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pointed+type"} pointed type
          %pre
            %code type ans = int option
          %p Now, our division routine can be written as follows in CPS:
          %pre
            %code
              :preserve
                let divide_cps : int -> int -> (int -> ans) -> ans =
                  fun x y k -> if y == 0 then None else k (x / y)          %p Observe that we have replaced the (somewhat misleading) original type
          %pre
            %code divide : int -&gt; int -&gt; int
          %p by the more complicated type
          %pre
            %code divide_cps : int -&gt; int -&gt; (int -&gt; ans) -&gt; ans
          %p
            This may be recognized as a form of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double+negation+translation"} double negation translation
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositions+as+types"} propositions as types
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/false"} absurdity
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⊥
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bot
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/empty+type"} empty
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+logic"} classical logic
            into
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/minimal+logic"} minimal logic
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊥
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bot
            behaves like a fixed atomic proposition.
          %h2#continuation_semantics Continuation semantics
          %p
            Whereas “continuation-passing style” refers to a programming technique, the term “continuation semantics” refers to a certain way of assigning
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/denotational+semantics"} denotational semantics
            to programs, originally in the context of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/domain+theory"} domain theory
            = succeed "." do
